# Gemini API key for server-side AI processing.
# The same key may instead live in config/api_keys.json as "gemini_api_key" —
# the file the desktop app and every action read. The environment wins when both are set.
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# ── The second Gemini key (optional, and worth setting) ───────────────────────
# Key 2 is the fallback. When Google stops key 1 for the day (a 429), JARVIS
# moves the question to key 2 and answers on — one question, one call, the retry
# costs nothing extra. It may instead live in config/api_keys.json as
# "gemini_api_key_2", which is what Settings → "The Brain" writes.
# GEMINI_API_KEY_2=

# ── Model names (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Leave these unset and the app connects to gemini-3.8-flash — Google's current
# stable Flash model (GA 2 Sep 2026, no shutdown announced; verified against
# ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/models on 12 Oct 2026). If that name is ever
# withdrawn the app steps back onto Google's own moving alias, gemini-flash-latest.
# Side tasks that only need a small, cheap model use gemini-3.5-flash-lite, the
# current stable Lite model.
# SUPPER_TEXT_MODEL is the application's setting; GEMINI_MODEL is honoured too.
# Legacy Gemini 2.5 text overrides are automatically migrated to gemini-3.8-flash.
# SUPPER_TEXT_MODEL=
# GEMINI_MODEL=
# SUPPER_IMAGE_MODEL=

# ── JARVIS's server voice (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# The page uses the computer's own voice by default (free, offline, no TTS quota).
# Gemini TTS is an explicit optional choice in Settings → Voice. These name the
# model and voice used only when the server voice is deliberately selected;
# leave them unset for the app's defaults.
# The voice is its own model, and its own setting — the text model is never
# used for speech. The app's default is gemini-3.8-flash-tts, Google's current
# flagship voice model (released 22 Sep 2026, no shutdown announced). If that
# name is ever withdrawn the app moves on to gemini-3.8-flash-lite-tts by
# itself — and ONLY for a withdrawn model name: a refused key, a spent quota or
# a dead connection is reported as itself, never hidden behind another model.
#
# What this replaced: gemini-2.5-flash-preview-tts, gemini-2.5-pro-preview-tts
# and gemini-3.1-flash-tts-preview are scheduled to shut down on 17 November 2026.
# Any legacy setting naming one of them is automatically migrated to the current
# stable model now, and Settings explains which old value was upgraded.
# SUPPER_VOICE_MODEL=gemini-3.8-flash-tts
# SUPPER_VOICE_NAME=Charon

# ── The daily Gemini warning level (optional) ─────────────────────────────────
# How many calls a local day should reach before JARVIS starts saying so in
# answers. The counter lives in runtime/quota.json and is kept on both keys — it
# is an early warning, NOT a ceiling: this app's own count never blocks a call,
# because a limit invented here cannot know Google's real limit. What does stop
# Gemini is Google's own answer (a 429), and even then JARVIS goes offline and
# carries on locally instead of failing. Unset means 20. 0 means "never warn".
# The old name SUPPER_GEMINI_DAILY_BUDGET is still read, but it no longer means
# a block.
# SUPPER_GEMINI_DAILY_WARN=

# ── Offline mode by hand (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# 1 makes JARVIS answer only from this computer from the moment it starts: no
# web search, no grounded research, no image or voice call, and nothing counted
# against any quota. Everything else keeps working — commands, local knowledge,
# reminders, drafting, the My Hands preview. The dashboard switch and the chat
# commands "go offline" / "come back online" do the same thing while it runs
# (runtime/offline.json). Offline also happens by itself when Google answers 429
# for every key, and lifts at local midnight.
# SUPPER_OFFLINE=

# ── The voice's own count (optional, warns only) ──────────────────────────────
# Spoken sentences per local day to count for the server voice, in its OWN file
# (runtime/voice_quota.json) so reading answers aloud never muddies the day's
# answer count. Unset means 400. Like the answer count, this WARNS and never
# blocks: the voice is never refused because of it. A sentence already spoken is
# replayed from memory and costs nothing.
# SUPPER_GEMINI_VOICE_BUDGET=

# ── The local knowledge base (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Answers kept on this computer in runtime/knowledge.json — the same file the
# desktop app uses. A question it can answer never reaches Gemini.
#
# Answers a model gave are kept with the model that produced them. An entry from
# a different model — or from before the app recorded the model at all — does not
# answer in place of the current model: the model is asked first, and the stored
# words are used only if it cannot be reached, said plainly to be out of date.
# Nothing is deleted: a replaced answer moves to the file's archive list.
# SUPPER_KNOWLEDGE_LEARN=off     # stop keeping Gemini's answers (existing ones still work)
# SUPPER_KNOWLEDGE_DAYS=30       # how long a kept Gemini answer lasts

# ── What works with no internet (there is no offline generative AI) ───────────
# Offline, JARVIS still runs: local commands (time, notes, reminders, system
# status, the rooms), the starter and taught answers in the knowledge base, the
# computer's own voice in the browser and through pyttsx3/Kokoro, and the
# "Hey Jarvis" wake word. Fresh, open-ended answers need Gemini — no on-device
# language model is integrated or tested. core/llm_client.py is a client for
# Ollama/LM Studio that nothing imports yet; wiring it in would need the owner
# to install one of those and download a model first.

# ── Tests only (leave unset in normal use) ────────────────────────────────────
# SUPPER_GEMINI_BASE_URL=      # point the Gemini client at a stub
# SUPPER_DASHBOARD_PORT=       # the dashboard's port, default 3000
